{
  "id": "COMPOUND:23881 - PROTEIN:5865",
  "verbalization": "Erythraline is shown to have a target relationship with SK-VLB through multiple pathways, all of which converge on the molecule MIP as a common target. The evidence suggests that Erythraline interacts with MIP, which is a target of several structurally diverse compounds, including 2-[4-[3-(3,4-Dimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan-6-yl]-3-methoxyphenoxy]-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5-triol and 5-Hydroxy-6,8-Dimethoxy-2,3-Dimethylbenzo[G]Chromen-4-One. These compounds all share the same target SK-VLB, indicating a potential mechanism of action involving MIP. The consistent presence of MIP as a target across different compounds supports a shared biological function, possibly involving the modulation of SK-VLB activity. The evidence suggests that Erythraline may act through a mechanism that involves interaction with MIP, which is a key target for multiple compounds, and that this interaction could contribute to the observed effects on SK-VLB. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Erythraline in modulating SK-VLB activity through a common target, supported by multiple lines of evidence.",
